# Line endings, declared once — for every machine, every editor and every script.
#
# Until 2026-09-21 this repository had no .gitattributes at all, so what ended a line
# was decided by each machine's core.autocrlf and by whatever tool happened to write
# the file. The result was two populations of source: 470 blobs stored LF and 28 stored
# CRLF (the 2023-era library sources, the .sln/.csproj set, and the agent journal).
# A tool that assumed one got the other wrong about one time in twenty, and the cost
# landed at the merge rather than at the edit: a file whose every line differs from
# main's conflicts WHOLE, so the two or three real changes had to be dug back out of a
# conflict spanning the entire file. That happened on nearly every merge into main.
#
# Worse, GameplayScreen.Session.cs had picked up two \r\r\n sequences from a script
# that wrote one ending into a file that used the other. A lone CR makes git classify
# a file as BINARY — it was the only .cs in the repo git would not diff or merge by
# line at all, and nothing said so.
#
# LF is the choice because 470 of the 498 text blobs already were LF (CRLF would have
# meant rewriting 470 files instead of 28), because every tool writes LF unless told
# otherwise, and because .claude/hooks/*.sh must be LF or bash refuses the shebang —
# and one of those hooks is what refuses `git reset --hard` in this repository.
#
# eol=lf sets the WORKING TREE too, not just the stored blob, deliberately: a uniform
# file on disk is one a script cannot half-convert. Editors here (Visual Studio, Rider,
# VS Code) all write LF happily on Windows, and git's clean filter folds CRLF back to
# LF anyway, so a tool that still writes CRLF costs nothing and shows no diff.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Sources, markup and configuration — always text, never guessed.
*.cs          text eol=lf
*.fx          text eol=lf
*.fxh         text eol=lf
*.json        text eol=lf
*.md          text eol=lf
*.txt         text eol=lf
*.csproj      text eol=lf
*.sln         text eol=lf
*.mgcb        text eol=lf
*.spritefont  text eol=lf
*.svg         text eol=lf
*.yml         text eol=lf
*.ps1         text eol=lf
*.py          text eol=lf
.editorconfig text eol=lf
.gitignore    text eol=lf
.gitattributes text eol=lf

# Shell scripts MUST be LF: a CRLF shebang makes bash fail with a message that names
# neither the file nor the reason. .claude/hooks/guard-destructive-git.sh is one.
*.sh          text eol=lf

# Media and compiled content — stored byte for byte, never diffed, never converted.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.ico  binary
*.ttf  binary
*.ogg  binary
*.wav  binary
*.xnb  binary

# Model assets (.x, .dae, .obj, .mtl) are TEXT and are treated as such by the rule at
# the top, deliberately. Marking them `binary` was tried first, to leave pipeline input
# nobody edits alone — and it did the opposite: `binary` freezes the WORKING TREE copy,
# which autocrlf had smudged to CRLF, so the stored blobs grew instead of staying put.
# Every one of them is stored LF today, so treating them as text keeps them byte for
# byte identical, and the content build compiles all of them on the way to the gates.
